// File handles are a **reference type** (Phase 5.2b): an external, stateful resource with identity,
// not value-semantic data. A handle is mutated in place through its methods (the cursor advances on
// `read_line`/`read`, the buffer grows on `write`) — even through an *immutable* binding — and that
// state is **shared** by every binding that aliases it. So aliasing a reader (`alias = reader`) and
// reading through either alias advances the one shared cursor: the three reads below yield three
// successive lines, not the same line thrice. This is deliberate — value semantics is for data;
// resources (files, sockets, streams) have reference semantics, as in Swift (`FileHandle` is a
// class), Haskell (`Handle` in `IO`), and Clojure. Both backends agree by construction.
// expect: stdout "alpha"
// expect: stdout "beta"
// expect: stdout "gamma"
// expect: stdout "<eof>"
// expect: exit 0
use std.{fs};

out = fs.open("notes.txt", "w");
out.write("alpha\nbeta\ngamma\n");
out.close();

reader = fs.open("notes.txt", "r");
alias = reader;
// Reads alternate between the two aliases; the shared cursor advances regardless of which is used.
echo reader.read_line() ?? "<eof>";
echo alias.read_line() ?? "<eof>";
echo reader.read_line() ?? "<eof>";
echo alias.read_line() ?? "<eof>";
